module Darklang.Cli.Docs.HttpServer

let content(): String =
  """# HTTP Server

## Start
  serve MyApp.router                        # --port defaults to 8080
  serve MyApp.router --port 9090 --max-body-bytes 1048576
  serve MyApp.router --live                 # follows your edits; an open page reloads
  # `http-server` is an alias for `serve`. Flags may come in any order.

The argument is the fully-qualified name of a function in the package tree, not a
file. It must be `Stdlib.Http.Request -> Stdlib.Http.Response`.

With `--live`, every request runs the newest router that passes its checks, and
a broken save keeps the previous one serving. Without it the version resolved at
start serves until you restart. Each request is its own
process: a slow handler holds up nobody; one past `http.requestTimeoutMs` (30 s
unset) is cancelled and answers 504; one that raises answers 500 with its error;
one stopped from `ps cancel` answers 503. `docs live` has the rest.

## A router

Handlers are values; `routeRequest` dispatches to them by method and path.

  module MyApp

  let helloFn(req: Stdlib.Http.Request): Stdlib.Http.Response =
    Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, "Hello, World!")

  val hello =
    Stdlib.HttpServer.Handler
      { route = "/hello"
        method = "GET"
        handler = helloFn }

  val handlers = [ hello ]

  // This is what you pass to `serve`. It has to be a named function.
  let router(req: Stdlib.Http.Request): Stdlib.Http.Response =
    Stdlib.HttpServer.routeRequest(handlers, req)

## Request

  type Stdlib.Http.Request =
    { method: String, url: String, headers: List<String * String>, body: Bytes }

  req.method                            # "GET", "POST", ...

The path is not a field - it's derived from the url:

  Stdlib.HttpServer.getPath(req)        # "/users/42"
  Stdlib.Http.parseQueryString(req.url)  # query string -> Dict

`body` is a Bytes. For text or JSON, convert first:

  let text = Stdlib.Utf8.decodeWithReplacement(req.body)

## Response

Status first, then the body. Headers, when you give them, go in between.

  Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, "Hello")
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithHtml(200, "<h1>Hi</h1>")
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithJson(200, jsonString)
  Stdlib.Http.responseWithHeaders(200, [ ("x-thing", "value") ], bodyBytes)
  Stdlib.Http.response(200, bodyBytes)  # the raw one; body is a Bytes
  Stdlib.Http.notFound()

A status is a plain number here. `Stdlib.Http.Status` names the common ones and
checks the rest: `Status.toInt64(Status.tooManyRequests)` is 429, and
`Status.fromInt64(n)` is an Error outside 100 to 599.

## Route params

  # given route "/user/:id" and a request for "/user/42"
  match Stdlib.HttpServer.getPathParam(req, "/user/:id", "id") with
  | Some(id) -> Stdlib.Http.responseWithText(200, $"user {id}")
  | None -> Stdlib.Http.notFound()

`matchRoute pattern path` is the lower-level form. It returns a record, not an
Option: `{ matched: Bool, vars: List<String * String> }`.

## A worked example

`Darklang.DemoData.HttpServerTest` is a small server that runs - root, /hello and
a POST /echo. Read it with `view Darklang.DemoData.HttpServerTest.router`.
"""
